Subject: [PATCH 2/3] metal: faster single-row Q2_0 mul_mv kernel

kernel_mul_mv_q2_0_f32 gives each thread 16 consecutive weights of a
block and reads the qs, qh and scales bytes of the block one at a time
for every weight, with the codebook in a constant array. That makes
single-token decode (n = 1) slow.

Four threads now share one 64-weight block; thread t handles weights
16*g + 4*t + c, so every byte of qs, qh and scales is loaded by exactly
one thread, as 16-bit words, and the 3-bit indices are extracted for
four weights at once with 32-bit shifts and masks. The codebook is
looked up from two packed 32-bit constants with a signed extract_bits()
and the activations are kept in registers as float4. N_R0_Q2_0 goes
from 8 to 4 rows per simdgroup. Like the other mul_mv kernels it reads
src1 as float4, so it assumes a 16-byte aligned, contiguous F32 src1 row
(the row stride is a multiple of 256 bytes when ne10 is a multiple of
64). Only kernel_mul_mv_q2_0_f32 and the _id variant that wraps it
change; the dequantizers, mul_mv_ext (n = 2..8) and mul_mm paths are
untouched.

Measured on an M1 Max, median of 3 runs of
  test-backend-ops perf -b MTL0 -o MUL_MAT,MUL_MAT_ID -p type_a=q2_0
(us per run, before -> after):
  MUL_MAT m=2688 n=1 k=2688                  137.7 ->  52.8
  MUL_MAT m=8192 n=1 k=2688                  290.1 -> 143.9
  MUL_MAT m=4096 n=1 k=14336                 655.9 -> 333.2
  MUL_MAT_ID 128 experts, 6 used, n=1, 1856x2688   386.6 -> 196.0
  MUL_MAT_ID 128 experts, 6 used, n=1, 2688x1856   395.2 -> 191.5
  MUL_MAT m=2688 k=2688 n=2,3,4,5,8,512: 76.1/85.0/89.7/100.8/161.6/
  1635.0 -> 75.3/85.2/90.1/101.0/161.4/1626.4 (no change)
With N_R0_Q2_0 = 8 and the new kernel, m=2688 n=1 takes 68.9 us, so 4
is kept. In the 'before' set one of the 3 runs was 1.7-1.9x slower on every
case; the medians are not affected. All 'after' runs agree within 1%.

test-backend-ops -b MTL0 -p q2_0 passes 202/202 with the tests commit
before this one (188 cases with quantizer-made blocks, including m=257
so the row count is not a multiple of the 4 rows per simdgroup or the 8
per threadgroup, and 14 cases with random raw blocks). Changing
hb = t >> 1 to hb = t & 1 in the new kernel leaves 84 of the 202
passing (exit 1).

Second step, same two kernels: a threadgroup pair-lookup table. The
per-weight codebook work above (select between two packed constants,
signed bit-field extract, int-to-float conversion) still dominated, so
the thread layout is kept and that work is replaced by a small table:

- A 64-entry float2 table in threadgroup memory (512 bytes), filled at
  kernel start. Entry i holds (cb[i & 7], cb[i >> 3]), the codebook
  values of two neighbouring weights. Every simdgroup writes the whole
  table, then one unconditional threadgroup_barrier, the same pattern
  the IQ4_NL kernel uses.
- The byte-packed 3-bit indices of 4 weights are merged pairwise with
  (ix | (ix >> 5)) & 0x003F003F, so 4 weights cost two table reads.
- The H >> hb and scale shifts are hoisted out of the group loop; the
  four 4 + nibble sub-block scales become one float4 applied with dot().
- qs / qh / scales are loaded as packed_ushort2 (alignment 2, legal on
  the 2-byte-aligned 30-byte block).
- The plain mul_mv entry point gains the threadgroup memory argument,
  and smem is set for Q2_0 in ggml_metal_library_get_pipeline_mul_mv
  and ..._mul_mv_id.

Measured on an M1 Max, test-backend-ops perf -b MTL0 (us per run,
first step -> with the table):
  MUL_MAT_ID 6 of 128 experts, 2688 -> 1856, n = 1   199.3 -> 91.5
  MUL_MAT_ID 6 of 128 experts, 1856 -> 2688, n = 1   191.9 -> 91.9
  MUL_MAT 2688 -> 3712, n = 1                         73.1 -> 34.9
In the same runs IQ4_NL takes 85.7 / 86.3 us and Q4_0 60.8 / 63.0 us on
the two expert ops. Alternatives measured: table-free SWAR 131 / 127 us,
plain 8-entry float table 99 us, 16 KB tables 50 us or more slower.
With the table: test-backend-ops -b MTL0 MUL_MAT 1177/1177 and
MUL_MAT_ID 802/802; test-quantize-fns passes. Decoded weights are
exactly those of dequantize_row_q2_0; float additions are in a
different order than in the first step, so results differ in the last
bits.

